node_modules/
*.log
.DS_Store
.wrangler/
dist/

# Android
apps/android/.gradle/
apps/android/build/
apps/android/app/build/
apps/android/local.properties
# Kotlin 2.x writes session files here during every build and removes them
# after. Transient — but /update refuses a dirty tree, and `git status
# --porcelain` counts untracked files, so a build in flight would stop a
# checkout host updating. Same reasoning as *.jks below.
apps/android/.kotlin/

# google-services.json is NOT ignored, deliberately. It was, from before there
# was a Firebase project, and leaving it that way would have been a silent
# failure of the kind this project keeps paying for: the Google Services plugin
# is applied only when the file is present, so CI would have built a release
# APK with no Firebase in it and push would simply never work in the shipped
# app. Nothing would have failed. Nothing would have said anything.
#
# It is not a secret. It ships inside every APK, so it is public the moment the
# app is, and its API key is restricted to this package. What sends
# notifications is the service account, which is a real secret and is not here.

# iOS — generated from project.yml by xcodegen
apps/ios/*.xcodeproj/
apps/ios/build/
# XcodeGen writes both of these on every `xcodegen generate` — "Plists are
# created on disk on every generation of the project". Committing one means
# editing a file whose contents are discarded before they are ever compiled.
# The source of truth for both is project.yml.
apps/ios/Fleetwright/Info.plist
apps/ios/Fleetwright/Fleetwright.entitlements

# A signing key must never be committed — but the reason it is ignored rather
# than merely not-added is /update: it refuses to pull into a dirty tree, and
# `git status --porcelain` counts untracked files. A stray release.jks in
# /opt/agent-fleet would silently stop that host updating.
*.jks

# IDE state, not project configuration. Android Studio rewrites .idea on
# almost every interaction and bakes in absolute local paths — the committed
# workspace.xml pinned an AVD at /Users/<someone>/.android/avd — so it
# conflicts on contact and is worthless on any other machine.
.idea/
*.iml
.DS_Store

# Compiled from src/web/console/*.jsx by scripts/build-web.mjs. The tests import
# from here because node cannot import JSX; `npm test` builds it first.
build/

# V8's raw coverage dump, which is not the coverage this repository measures.
# `scripts/check-coverage.mjs` writes its lcov into a mkdtemp directory and
# ratchets against test/coverage-floor.json, so nothing here ever wants a
# coverage/ in the tree — but NODE_V8_COVERAGE lands wherever a contributor's
# shell or editor points it, and that is how two files of node-internal byte
# offsets, covering not one line of this project, reached main.
#
# Ignored rather than merely deleted, for the reason *.jks is above: /update
# refuses to pull into a dirty tree and `git status --porcelain` counts
# untracked files, so a coverage run left behind in /opt/agent-fleet would
# silently stop that host updating.
coverage/

# ERROR REPORTING AUTH TOKENS. A DSN is public and lives in the manifests; these
# are not — they upload debug symbols and ProGuard mappings, and each one can
# write to the Sentry project. The wizards create them and add them here; this
# repository refuses them up front rather than after the first accidental
# commit, because a token in a public history is a token to rotate.
apps/android/sentry.properties
apps/ios/.sentryclirc
.sentryclirc
sentry.properties

# Real secrets for `wrangler dev` and the deploy-button flow. The example file
# is the one that ships — it declares which secrets the Deploy to Cloudflare
# dialog asks for. This pair of patterns is wrangler's own convention.
.dev.vars*
!.dev.vars.example
